WEBVTT
TITLE Linearity of Expectation: Sums, Indicators and the Counts GATE Asks For — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Two dice: 36 equally likely outcomes. The sum runs from 2 to 12.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Averaging all 36 sums gives 7. That is the brute-force route.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
One die averages 3.5, so two dice average 3.5 + 3.5 = 7.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
That is the whole idea: the mean of a sum is the sum of the means.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The two dice were independent, but linearity never used that fact.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Let X = Y: one die, copied. The joint table collapses onto its diagonal.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
E[X + Y] is still 3.5 + 3.5 = 7, and 2X really does average 7.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
But E[X·Y] is 91/6 = 15.17, not 12.25. Products do need independence.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
An indicator is 1 when its event happens and 0 when it does not.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Its mean is the probability: E[I] = 1·p + 0·(1−p) = p.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
A count of twelve events is the sum of twelve indicators.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
So the expected count is the sum of twelve probabilities — no joint distribution.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Shuffle n cards: a fixed point is a card that lands back in its own place.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
All 5040 shuffles of seven cards contain 5040 fixed points in total: one each.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
By indicators: n events of probability 1/n, so the mean is n · (1/n) = 1 for all n.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The n = 5 distribution: 44 shuffles have none, 45 have one, and four is impossible.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
An inversion is a pair out of order: for n = 5 the average over 120 shuffles is 5.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Each of the C(n,2) pairs is inverted in exactly half the permutations.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So the mean is C(n,2)/2 = n(n−1)/4, which is 5 at n = 5 and 3 at n = 4.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The pairs are dependent — one swap changes many — and linearity does not care.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
n keys into m slots, each key equally likely to land in any slot.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
One slot holds a sum of n indicators, so its expected load is n/m.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Each of the C(n,2) key pairs collides with probability 1/m, so E[collisions] = C(n,2)/m.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
GATE 2022 used one hash function for odd keys and another for even: the answer stayed n/m.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Randomised quicksort compares each pair at most once, so count comparisons by pairs.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Keys i and j are compared exactly when the first pivot among them is one of the two.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
That probability is 2/(j−i+1), and summing over pairs gives 2(n+1)H(n) − 4n.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
At n = 10 the exact expectation is 24.44 comparisons, under the 2n ln n = 46.05 bound.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Linearity is a property of the mean only. Variance does not inherit it.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Var(X+Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2Cov(X,Y); independence removes only the last term.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
With X = Y the sum is 2X, so the variance is 4 times Var X = 11.67, not 5.83.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
And the mean of a function is not the function of the mean: E[1/X] = 0.408, 1/E[X] = 0.286.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
For a non-negative integer variable there is a second route: add the tail probabilities.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Each value k is counted exactly k times, which is why the two sums agree.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Larger of two dice: 1 + 35/36 + 32/36 + 27/36 + 20/36 + 11/36.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
That sum is 161/36 = 4.472, and the 36-outcome average agrees exactly.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Write the quantity as a sum of indicators. That is the whole procedure.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Ask of each indicator only: what is the probability of its event?

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Trap: thinking independence is needed. Trap: applying linearity to variance or to products.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Trap: rejecting a fractional answer. An expected count need not be a count.

